    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d354">

                <pb facs="d353-3" n="3" sameAs="frus:frus1969-76ve10/pgd353-3"/>

                <head>354. Memorandum From the Acting Inspector General of Foreign Assistance of the
                    Department of State (Faunce) to Secretary of Defense Laird<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d354-f1"/>
                    <anchor n="2" xml:id="d354-f2"/>
                </head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>Washington</placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1971-08-03">August 3, 1971</date>
                    </dateline></opener>
                <pb facs="d354-1" n="1" xml:id="pgd354-1"/>
                <p>Dear Mr. Secretary:</p>
                <p>I am attaching a comprehensive report by Inspectors Briggs, Markey and Clark on
                    their inspection of the MAP in Guatemala.</p>
                <p>The lack of planning indicated by the failure to fill one important post for over
                    seven months and the failure to provide an overlapping term in another post is
                    most disturbing.</p>
                <p>This and certain other findings suggest strongly that the backup support of what
                    appears to be a good MILGP needs to be strengthened materially.</p>
                <p>Please let me have your comments as soon as possible.</p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#bold">Anthony Faunce</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d354-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d354-f1-content">
                <p>Source: Washington National Records Center, OASD/ISA Files: FRC 330–74A–0083, 19,
                    Guatemala 1971 000.1. Confidential. In the attached July 29 memorandum to Acting
                    Inspector General Faunce, inspectors William T. Briggs, Edward W. Markey, and
                    Bruce Clark noted that the position of Chief of USMILGP/Guatemala had been
                    vacant since December 18, 1970, because “the problem of terrorism in Guatemala
                    City may have influenced the officers’ decision not to accept the assignment out
                    of concern for the safety of their families.” The attached memorandum is not
                    published.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d354-f2" n="2" xml:id="d354-f2-content">
                <p>Faunce summarized a report on the U.S. Military Group in Guatemala, noting a
                    disturbing failure to fill one important post for over seven months.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
